ecking the services with \'kubectl get services\' could show if the services are exposed properly.\n\nBut the user already ran \'kubectl get deployments\' and saw that \'geo\' is not ready. The next logical step is to check the pods related to the \'geo\' service to see why it\'s not running. So, using \'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation\' would list all pods, and then I can look for any pods related to \'geo\' and check their status. If there are errors, that might indicate the root cause, like image pull failures, resource limits, or crashes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe checking the metrics with \'get_metrics\' could show if the \'geo\' service is receiving traffic or if there are any anomalies. But first, it\'s better to confirm the pod statuses. So, the next tool call should be to get the pods in the namespace to investigate further.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The deployment list shows the "geo" service is not ready (0/1). To diagnose this, I need to inspect the underlying pods for the "geo" service to check for errors, crashes, or resource issues. Running "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will reveal the current state of all pods, including any failures or restarts. This will help identify if the pod is stuck in a crash loop, has pending containers, or is otherwise unresponsive.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 447, 'prompt_tokens': 2099, 'total_tokens': 2546, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb38-3ecf-7f41-9862-2def4e5583a2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2099, 'output_tokens': 447, 'total_tokens': 2546}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwar