The tool to use here is "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd" with the command "kubectl logs geo-57c7bd6d5c-p6fr5 -n hotel-reservation". This will fetch the logs for the specific pod, which can help identify the root cause of the failure. If the logs show an error, like a crash or a configuration issue, that would point us in the right direction. If the logs aren't available, maybe the pod has crashed and the logs have been lost, which would require checking the events or the deployment configuration.

Alternatively, if the logs don't show anything, we might need to check the pod's events using "kubectl describe pod" to see if there are any events related to the pod's failure, such as image pull errors, startup failures, or resource issues. But since the user has a limited number of tool calls, it's best to first check the logs, as that's the most direct way to see what's going wrong.
