dn\'t return any secrets, maybe the secrets are not in the default namespace or the user didn\'t specify the correct namespace. Wait, the namespace is hotel-reservation, so the command should be correct. Alternatively, maybe the secrets are not yet created or there\'s a typo. \n\nThe user needs to check the specific secret that the geo service is using. If the secret isn\'t found, that could mean the credentials are not properly set up. Alternatively, if the secret exists but has incorrect data, that would also cause the authentication failure. \n\nSo the next step is to describe the secret that the geo service is using. The user should look for a secret related to "geo" and "mongodb" in the hotel-reservation namespace. If the secret isn\'t present, that\'s a problem. If it is present, they need to check its contents. \n\nBut since the previous command didn\'t return any secrets, maybe the user needs to check the secret\'s name again. Perhaps the secret was created with a different name, like "mongo-geo-auth" or something similar. Alternatively, maybe the secret is in a different namespace. \n\nWait, the user is in the hotel-reservation namespace, so the command should be correct. If there are no secrets, that\'s a problem. But the geo service must have some way to connect to MongoDB, so the secret should exist. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user needs to check the deployment or pod\'s environment variables to see which secret is being referenced. For example, the geo service might be using an environment variable like MONGO_USER or MONGO_PASSWORD that points to a secret. \n\nBut since the user can\'t run commands like "kubectl describe" on the pod, they might need to check the deployment\'s YAML to see which secret is being used. However, the available tools don\'t include "kubectl describe" for deployments. \n\nWait, the user has the "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd" tool. They can run "kubectl get secrets" to list all secrets in the namespace. If there are no secrets, 