e check the Deployment YAML again. \n\nIn the Deployment YAML, the user added environment variables for MONGO_USER, MONGO_PASS, and MONGO_AUTHDB, pointing to the Secret. But maybe the Secret's keys are named differently. For example, the Secret's data keys are username, password, and authdb. The Deployment is using the same keys, so that should be correct. \n\nWait, the Secret was created with the keys username, password, and authdb. The Deployment's env variables are correctly referencing those keys. So the Secret should be properly mounted. \n\nBut the pod is still crashing. Maybe the MongoDB connection is failing for another reason, like incorrect credentials, network issues, or the MongoDB service not being available. \n\nThe user could check the MongoDB pod's status to ensure it's running. They can use 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation' to see if the MongoDB pod is up. If it's not, that's another issue. \n\nAlternatively, check the logs of the 'geo' pod for any specific error messages. Since the previous attempt to get logs didn't work, maybe the user should try again or use a different command. \n\nWait, the user tried 'kubectl logs --previous' but maybe the pod hasn't been restarted yet. The pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, so it's restarting repeatedly. The logs from the last crash might not be available. \n\nIn that case, the next step should be to check the MongoDB pod's logs to see if there's any authentication failure on the MongoDB side. The user can use 'kubectl logs' on the MongoDB pod to see if it's rejecting the connection from 'geo'. \n\nAlternatively, check the metrics using Prometheus to see if the 'geo' service is making any requests to MongoDB and if there are any errors. \n\nBut given the tools available, the user can use 'get_metrics' with a PromQL query to check for errors or connection issues. For example, querying for MongoDB connection errors or failed requests. \n\nHowever, the user might not have access to Prometheus metrics. So, the best next step is to check the MongoDB pod's logs to see if the authentication is failing. \n\nSo, the user should run 'kubectl logs <mongodb-pod-name> -n hotel-reservation' to check the MongoDB logs for any authentication errors. \n\nBut the user doesn't know the name of the MongoDB pod. They can first get the list of pods in the namespace to find the MongoDB pod. \n\nSo, the steps would be: \n1. Get the list of pods in the 'hotel-reservation' namespace to find the MongoDB pod. \n2. Check the MongoDB pod's logs for any errors related to authentication. \n3. Based on the logs, determine if the credentials are correct or if there's a network issue. \n\nAlternatively, if the MongoDB pod is not running, that's another problem. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to identify the MongoDB pod and check its status.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8041af61ee602f78', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 975, 'prompt_tokens': 7024, 'total_tokens': 7999, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb3a-eda1-7e11-8b24-3b6a55df1b04-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8041af61ee602f78', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7024, 'output_tokens': 975, 'total_tokens': 7999}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-xzw4t                   1/1     Running            0               5m9s\nfrontend-6df8