lled \'mongodb-geo-auth\' and tried to inject the credentials into the Deployment, but the pod is still crashing.\n\nFirst, I need to check why the latest changes aren\'t working. The user applied a new Deployment configuration that includes the MONGO_URI environment variable using the Secret values. But the pod is still in CrashLoopBackOff. The logs from the previous attempt showed that the app wasn\'t using the MONGO_URI, which suggests that the environment variable might not be correctly set or the application isn\'t reading it properly.\n\nWait, in the last tool call, the user used a command to apply the Deployment with MONGO_URI set as "mongodb://$(MONGO_USER):$(MONGO_PASS)@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=$(MONGO_AUTHDB)". But in Kubernetes, environment variables in the Deployment are referenced using ${VAR_NAME}, not $(VAR_NAME). So the correct syntax should be "mongodb://${MONGO_USER}:${MONGO_PASS}@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=${MONGO_AUTHDB}". Using $( instead of ${ might cause the variable to not be expanded correctly, leading to the app using the wrong URI or default values, which would result in authentication failure.\n\nSo the root cause here is the incorrect use of $( instead of ${ in the MONGO_URI value. The application is probably not receiving the correct credentials, leading to the SASL error again. To fix this, the Deployment should use the correct syntax for environment variables. Additionally, the user should check if the Secret values (username, password, authdb) are correctly base64 encoded and match what the MongoDB server expects. Also, ensuring that the MongoDB service is reachable and the authSource is correct is important.\n\nThe next step is to correct the MONGO_URI in the Deployment to use ${ instead of $(), apply the updated Deployment, and then check the pod logs again to confirm if the credentials are being used correctly. If the issue persists, they might need to verify the Secret\'s contents or check the MongoDB server\'s configuration for authentication settings.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The `geo` pod is still failing, and the latest attempt used `$(MONGO_USER)` instead of `${MONGO_USER}` in the `MONGO_URI` environment variable. Kubernetes requires `${VAR_NAME}` syntax for environment variable expansion. This mistake likely caused the app to use incorrect or default credentials, leading to the SASL authentication failure. Correcting this syntax and reapplying the Deployment will ensure the app uses the proper credentials from the Secret.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: geo\\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: hotel-reserv-geo\\n        env:\\n        - name: MONGO_URI\\n          value: \\"mongodb://${MONGO_USER}:${MONGO_PASS}@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=${MONGO_AUTHDB}\\"\\n        - name: MONGO_USER\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: username\\n        - name: MONGO_PASS\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: password\\n        - name: MONGO_AUTHDB\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: authdb\\nEOF"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 821, 'prompt_tokens': 8750, 'total_tokens': 9571, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb3c-a424-7361-b720-c4051098cf08-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8750, 'output_tokens': 821, 'total_