sted to parse the environment variable. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the app is using a different method to get the credentials, like reading them from a config file that's not being updated. But given the tools available, the user can only adjust the deployment configuration. \n\nSo the next step is to verify that the MONGO_URI is correctly set in the pod's environment. The user can check the pod's environment variables to confirm. If that's not possible, maybe the app needs to be modified to use the URI, but since the user can't change the code, they might need to ensure the URI is correctly formatted and that the Secret values are correct. \n\nWait, the user already checked the Secret values. They are correct. So the issue must be that the app isn't using the MONGO_URI. The only way to force the app to use it is to ensure the environment variable is correctly set and that the app is configured to read it. \n\nMaybe the app is using a different variable name, like MONGO_URL instead of MONGO_URI. But the logs show the app is reading MONGO_URI. \n\nAlternatively, the app might not be properly parsing the URI, leading to the credentials being omitted. In that case, the user might need to adjust the URI format or the app's configuration. \n\nSince the user can't change the app code, the best option is to ensure the MONGO_URI is correctly injected and that the app is using it. If that's not working, the problem might be in the app's code, but the user has to work with the tools available. \n\nSo the next step is to apply the deployment again, ensuring the MONGO_URI is correctly formatted, and then check the pod logs again to see if the URI is being used. If it's still not working, the user might need to consider other factors, like the app's configuration or the Secret's access permissions. \n\nBut given the tools available, the user should keep trying to apply the correct configuration and check the logs until the issue is resolved.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'too