low/go-micro-services/vendor/github.com/rs/zerolog/event.go:158 +0x2b8\ngithub.com/rs/zerolog.(*Event).Msg(...)\n\t/go/src/github.com/harlow/go-micro-services/vendor/github.com/rs/zerolog/event.go:110\nmain.initializeDatabase({0xc00002c288, 0xc0000cbdd0})\n\t/go/src/github.com/harlow/go-micro-services/cmd/geo/db.go:26 +0x167\nmain.main()\n\t/go/src/github.com/harlow/go-micro-services/cmd/geo/main.go:38 +0x877\n', id='60ff2718-7286-43f8-9e0e-70e3470ed169', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-876faf09b067dc5f'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4eb8298d-a1e1-4906-927e-d18179b759bf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with the \'geo\' service in their hotel reservation application. The problem is that the \'geo\' pod is crashing with an authentication error when trying to connect to the MongoDB database. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the authentication is failing. The logs show a SASL authentication error, which usually means the credentials provided are incorrect or the user doesn\'t have the right permissions. \n\nEarlier steps included creating a Secret called \'mongodb-geo-auth\' with username, password, and authdb. But the logs indicate that the \'geo\' service isn\'t using these credentials correctly. Maybe the environment variables aren\'t set properly in the Deployment. \n\nLooking back at the Deployment YAML, the user tried to add environment variables for MONGO_USER, MONGO_PASS, and MONGO_AUTHDB, referencing the Secret. But the pod is still failing. Perhaps the Secret wasn\'t applied correctly, or the keys in the Secret don\'t match what the app expects. \n\nWait, the Secret was created with keys \'username\', \'password\', and \'authdb\', but the app might be looking for different keys, like \'MONGO_USER\' as the key in the Secret. Or maybe the Secret wasn\'t mounted correctly in the Deployment. \n\nAnother angle: the MongoDB connection string in the app might be using the wrong credentials. The log shows the URL is \'mongodb-geo:27017\', which is the service name. But if the Secret isn\'t being used, the app might be using default or incorrect credentials. \n\nI should check if the Secret is correctly referenced in the Deployment. The previous command to update the Deployment might not have been applied correctly. Maybe the Deployment YAML had a typo or the Secret name was wrong. \n\nAlternatively, the app might require the credentials to be passed as part of the connection string, not as separate environment variables. Or the Secret might not be in the correct namespace. \n\nWait, the Secret was created in the \'hotel-reservation\' namespace, which matches the Deployment\'s namespace. So that\'s correct. \n\nAnother possibility: the Secret\'s data is encoded incorrectly. When creating the Secret, the user used base64 encoding for the username, password, and authdb. Let me verify that. \n\nThe Secret was created with:\nusername: Z2VvLXVzZXI= (which is \'geo_user\')\npassword: Z2VvLXByb2ZpbGVz (which is \'geo_password\')\nauthdb: YWRtaW4= (which is \'admin\')\n\nThese look correct. So the Secret should have the right data. \n\nBut the app is still failing. Maybe the environment variables in the Deployment are not correctly named. For example, the app might expect \'MONGO_USER\' to be the key in the Secret, but the Deployment is using \'username\' as the key. Wait, no—the Deployment\'s env variables are set to use the Secret\'s keys. Let me check the Deployment YAML again. \n\nIn the previous tool call, the user applied a Deployment that sets:\n- MONGO_USER from secretKeyRef with name \'mongodb-geo-auth\' and key \'username\'\n- MONGO_PASS from the same Secret with key \'password\'\n- MONGO_AUTHDB from the same Secret with key \'authdb\'\n\nSo the environment variables should be set correctly. \n\nBut the app is still getting an authentication error. Maybe the MongoDB service is expecting a different authentication mechanism. For example, maybe the user needs to specify the authentication database in the connection string, or the password is incorrect. \n\nAlternatively, the Secret might not be mounted properly. Let me check the pod\'s environment variables to see if they\'re set. \n\nWait, the user can\'t check that directly now. Maybe the next step is to verify the Secret\'s existence and contents again. \n\nAlternatively, the app might be using the wrong username or password. For example, the \'geo_user\' might not have the necessary permissions in MongoDB. \n\nBut the user has already created the Secret, so the credentials should be correct. \n\nWait, the error message says "SASL authentication failed." SASL is used for authentication in MongoDB, so the credentials must be correct. But maybe the user is missing the authentication database in the connection string. \n\nLooking at the log, the connection string is \'mongodb-geo:27017\', which doesn\'t include the authentication database. The app might need to include the authdb in the connection string, like \'mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=admin\'. \n\nIf the app is not passing the authSource parameter, the authentication might fail even with correct credentials. \n\nSo the root cause might be that the connection string is missing the authSource parameter, leading to the authentication failure. \n\nTo fix this, the app\'s configuration should include the authSource in the MongoDB connection string. The environment variables set earlier (MONGO_AUTHDB) might not be used correctly in the app\'s code. \n\nTherefore, the next step is to check the app\'s configuration to ensure that the authSource is included in the connection string. If that\'s not possible, the user might need to adjust the environment variables to include the authSource or modify the app\'s code to use the correct parameters. \n\nAlternatively, the app might be expecting the authdb to be passed as part of the connection string, so the environment variables might not be sufficient. \n\nIn this case, the user should verify how the app constructs the MongoDB connection string and ensure that the authSource is included. If the app is using the environment variables, the variables might not be correctly referenced. \n\nBut given the current information, the most likely fix is to ensure that the connection string includes the authSource parameter. So the user should update the app\'s configuration to include the authSource, possibly using the MONGO_AUTHDB environment variable. \n\nAlternatively, the Secret might need to include the authSource as part of the credentials, but that\'s not standard. \n\nIn any case, the immediate next step is to check the app\'s connection string configuration and ensure that the authSource is correctly specified. If that\'s not possible, the user might need to adjust the environment variables or the Secret to include the necessary parameters. \n\nSince the user can\'t modify the app\'s code directly, the best option is to ensure that the environment variables are correctly set and that the app is using them in the connection string. If that\'s not the case, the authentication will fail. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to check the environment variables in the pod to confirm they\'re set correctly. But since the user can\'t do that directly, they might need to check the Deployment again or adjust the connection string in the app\'s configuration. \n\nAlternatively, the user could try to update the Deployment to include the authSource in the connection string using the environment variables. \n\nBut given the current tools available, the user might need to re-examine the Deployment YAML to ensure that the environment variables are correctly set and that the app is using them. \n\nIn summary, the root cause is likely the missing authSource in the MongoDB connection string, leading to the SASL authentication failure. The solution is to ensure the connection string includes the authSource parameter, possibly using the MONGO_AUTHDB environment variable.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The `geo` service is failing due to a **SASL authentication error**, which indicates the MongoDB credentials are either incorrect or not properly configured in the connection string. The logs confirm the app is using the wrong credentials or missing the `authSource` parameter in the MongoDB connection string. To resolve this, we need to ensure the `geo` service uses the correct credentials from the `mongodb-geo-auth` Secret and includes the `authSource` in the connection string.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: geo\\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: hotel-reserv-geo\\n        env:\\n        - name: MONGO_URI\\n          value: \\"mongodb://\\$MONGO_USER:\\$MONGO_PASS@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=\\$MONGO_AUTHDB\\"\\n        - name: MONGO_USER\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: username\\n        - name: MONGO_PASS\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: password\\n        - name: MONGO_AUTHDB\\n          valueFrom:\\n            secretKeyRef:\\n              name: mongodb-geo-auth\\n              key: authdb\\nEOF"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1850, 'prompt_tokens': 7756, 'total_tokens': 9606, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb3b-6cfb-74b3-b1fe-79e08a18921a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7756, 'output_tokens': 1850, 'total_tokens': 9606}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3cf08ccc-2007-4a1a-8b64-690f6986f535'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where the \'geo\' service is failing to connect to the MongoDB database. From the logs, it\'s clear that there\'s an authentication error. The previous steps showed that the \'geo\' pod was crashing because the SASL authentication failed. \n\nFirst, the user checked the Kubernetes Secrets in the namespace and found that the \'mongodb-geo-auth\' Secret was missing. They created it with the necessary credentials, but the pod still wasn\'t starting. Then, they tried to update the Deployment to use the Secret\'s environment variables, but the pod kept crashing.\n\nLooking at the latest logs, the error is about SASL authentication failing. The user\'s last attempt was to set environment variables for MONGO_URI, MONGO_USER, MONGO_PASS, and MONGO_AUTHDB. However, the MongoDB connection string might still be missing the \'authSource\' parameter, which is required for authentication. The error message mentions \'authSource=$MONGO_AUTHDB\', but maybe the environment variable isn\'t being substituted correctly, or the URI format is incorrect.\n\nWait, in the Deployment YAML, the MONGO_URI is set to "mongodb://$MONGO_USER:$MONGO_PASS@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=$MONGO_AUTHDB". The problem here is that the environment variables $MONGO_USER, $MONGO_PASS, and $MONGO_AUTHDB are not being expanded properly. In Kubernetes, environment variables in a pod\'s spec are referenced using ${VAR_NAME}, not $VAR_NAME. So the correct format should be "mongodb://${MONGO_USER}:${MONGO_PASS}@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=${MONGO_AUTHDB}".\n\nThat\'s the key mistake. The user used $ instead of ${, which means the variables aren\'t being substituted. The MongoDB connection string is still using the placeholder names instead of the actual values from the Secret. This would result in the connection string being incorrect, leading to authentication failure.\n\nSo the next step is to correct the MONGO_URI environment variable to use ${} syntax for proper substitution. Also, ensure that the \'authSource\' parameter is correctly included. Once this is fixed, the pod should use the correct credentials and auth source, allowing it to authenticate with MongoDB successfully.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl rollout restart deployment/geo -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-847cc7a668ce3aed', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 522, 'prompt_tokens': 9130, 'total_tokens': 9652, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb3b-c6b2-73e0-a5ef-592f1cb04a56-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl rollout restart deployment/geo -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-847cc7a668ce3aed', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9130, 'output_tokens': 522, 'total_tokens': 9652}), ToolMessage(content='deployment.apps/geo restarted\n', id='6763e722-0d68-465c-a245-6a2626ea948e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-847cc7a668ce3aed'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2d068f1c-87f4-4dec-8c2b-3e4579ea5d0c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the \'geo\' service in their hotel reservation application. So far, they\'ve created a Secret for MongoDB credentials and updated the Deployment to use those secrets. But the pod is still crashing with a SASL authentication error. \n\nLooking at the latest logs, the error is about the server returning an error on the SASL authentication step. The user tried setting environment variables for MONGO_URI, MONGO_USER, MONGO_PASS, and MONGO_AUTHDB. The MONGO_URI uses placeholders like $MONGO_USER and $MONGO_PASS, which should be replaced by the actual values from the Secret. Wait, but in Kubernetes, environment variables are injected as the actual values, not placeholders. So maybe the MONGO_URI is not correctly formatted. \n\nWait, the user\'s Deployment YAML sets MONGO_URI to "mongodb://$MONGO_USER:$MONGO_PASS@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=$MONGO_AUTHDB". But in Kubernetes, the environment variables are expanded at runtime, so the $MONGO_USER and $MONGO_PASS should be replaced by the actual values from the Secret. However, if the Secret\'s keys are named differently, like \'username\' instead of \'MONGO_USER\', that could cause issues. \n\nWait, the Secret was created with keys \'username\', \'password\', and \'authdb\'. The Deployment\'s env variables are referencing \'MONGO_USER\', \'MONGO_PASS\', and \'MONGO_AUTHDB\'. So the secretKeyRef is mapping the Secret\'s \'username\' to \'MONGO_USER\', which is correct. But the MONGO_URI is using $MONGO_USER and $MONGO_PASS, which should be replaced by the actual values. However, maybe the app expects the credentials to be in the URI directly, not as environment variables. Or perhaps the authSource is missing in the URI. \n\nWait, the error message says "server returned error on SASL authentication step: Authentication failed." This usually happens when the username or password is incorrect, or the authSource (database) is wrong. The user\'s Secret has \'authdb\' as the key, which is mapped to MONGO_AUTHDB. 