\'t being passed to the app. Could there be a typo in the env variable name? Like MONGO_URI vs MONGO_URL? \n\nLooking at the Deployment YAML, the env variable is named MONGO_URI. The app\'s code might be looking for a different name, like MONGO_URL. If that\'s the case, the app wouldn\'t read the MONGO_URI variable, leading to the default URI being used. \n\nAlternatively, the app might be using a different method to read the URI, like from a config file or a different environment variable. The user\'s logs show that the app is reading the URI from the environment, but if the variable isn\'t set, it defaults to mongodb-geo:27017. \n\nSo the next step should be to check the app\'s code or configuration to see how it\'s supposed to read the MongoDB URI. If the app expects a different environment variable name, like MONGO_URL, then changing the Deployment to use that name would fix the issue. \n\nAlternatively, if the app is supposed to use the username and password directly in the URI without the authSource, then the authSource parameter might not be needed. But the error is a SASL authentication failure, which usually requires the authSource. \n\nAnother angle: the Secret might not be correctly mounted. The user created the Secret, but maybe the Deployment isn\'t referencing it correctly. The Deployment\'s env variables are using the Secret, but perhaps the Secret is in a different namespace or the key names are mismatched. \n\nWait, the Secret is in the same namespace as the Deployment, hotel-reservation. The keys are username, password, authdb, which match the env variables. So that should be okay. \n\nGiven that the app is still using the default URI, the most likely issue is that the MONGO_URI environment variable isn\'t being read by the app. The user should check the app\'s code to see how it\'s supposed to read the URI. If the app is using a different variable name, that\'s the problem. \n\nAlternatively, the app might not be reading environment variables at all, and the URI is hardcoded. In that case, the solution would be to modify the app\'s code to use the environment variable. But since the user can\'t modify the app\'s code, maybe there\'s a different approach. \n\nWait, the user tried setting MONGO_URI with the authSource, but the logs show the URI is still missing that part. Maybe the app is not using the MONGO_URI variable. The user could try setting the URI directly in the env variable without using the other variables. For example, set MONGO_URI to the full URI with the credentials and authSource. \n\nBut the user already tried that. The logs still show the URI is not being used. Maybe the app is not reading the MONGO_URI variable. The user could check the pod\'s environment variables to see if MONGO_URI is present. \n\nSo the next step should be to describe the pod and check the environment variables. If MONGO_URI is missing, then the Deployment is not correctly setting it. If it\'s present but the app isn\'t using it, then the app\'s code is the issue. \n\nBut the user can\'t modify the app\'s code, so the only option is to ensure the environment variables are correctly set. The user should verify that the Deployment is correctly referencing the Secret and that the MONGO_URI is properly formatted. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the app expects the credentials to be in the URI directly, without separate variables. In that case, the user should construct the URI using the Secret values and set it as the MONGO_URI. \n\nWait, the user already tried that. The logs still show the URI is not being used. So perhaps the app is not reading the MONGO_URI variable. The user could try setting the URI in the env variable without using the other variables, like:\n\nMONGO_URI="mongodb://username:password@mongodb-geo:27017?authSource=admin"\n\nBut the user can\'t hardcode the credentials. They need to use the Secret. So the correct approach is to ensure the MONGO_URI is correctly constructed using the Secret values. \n\nGiven that