 not be reaching the app\'s configuration. Another possibility is that the app is not properly parsing the URI, perhaps due to a bug in the code that reads the environment variables.\n\nSince the user has tried multiple times to apply the correct environment variables and the issue persists, the next step is to check the actual environment variables inside the running pod. If the pod is running, they can exec into it and check the environment variables. But since the pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, they might need to wait for it to restart or check the previous logs again.\n\nWait, the user tried to get the logs of the previous pod, but the logs still show the same error. Maybe the app is not using the MONGO_URI at all. The user should verify if the environment variables are correctly set in the pod. If the variables are present but not used, the issue is with the app\'s code. If the variables are missing, then the Secret might not be correctly referenced.\n\nAnother angle: the Secret\'s keys might be named differently. The user used "username", "password", and "authdb" as keys in the Secret. The app might be expecting different keys, like "user", "pass", or "authSource". If the keys don\'t match, the environment variables won\'t be set correctly, leading to the app using default values or failing to connect.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the actual environment variables inside the pod. If the pod is running, they can exec into it and print the environment variables. If the pod isn\'t running, they might need to wait for it to start or check the deployment configuration again. Alternatively, they could try mounting the Secret as a volume and accessing the credentials directly, but that\'s more complex.\n\nGiven the time spent and the repeated failures, the most likely cause is either the app not using the MONGO_URI variable or the Secret keys not matching what the app expects. The user should verify the environment variables inside the pod to confirm they\'re set correctly. If they are, then the issue is with the app\'s code, and the user might need to adjust the configuration or fix the code to use the correct variables.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod -n hotel-reservation geo-7747cc8444-dnbw9"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8838228349af2e80', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 877, 'prompt_tokens': 14705, 'total_tokens': 15582, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb3f-439d-7742-abfa-b9b89a4bc922-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod -n hotel-reservation geo-7747cc8444-dnbw9'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8838228349af2e80', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 14705, 'output_tokens': 877, 'total_tokens': 15582}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             geo-7747cc8444-dnbw9\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.31\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 10:01:09 +0000\nLabels:           io.kompose.service=geo\n                  pod-template-hash=7747cc8444\nAnnotations:      kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                  kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                  kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt: 2026-03-17T10:01:08Z\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                    cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\nStatus:           Running\nIP:     